{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0d3479bb50074fd30169bb3ec018b88191bc6b0853f3ada46f", "created": 1773878976, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"RAI Therapy, Iodine Uptake, and Papillary/Oncocytic Thyroid Cancer Management\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community is a clinically focused network centered on radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy and the conditions under which it is used to manage differentiated thyroid cancers. The key linking concept is iodine uptake: when thyroid cancer (including metastases) can take up iodine, RAI-based imaging and treatment become relevant. Whole-body radioiodine scanning appears as an operational adjunct to RAI therapy, with noted variation by treatment center in whether scanning occurs pre-therapy, post-therapy, or both. Papillary thyroid cancer/carcinoma is represented with multiple subtypes (some described as potentially more aggressive), and radiation therapy is explicitly described as rarely used for papillary thyroid cancers. A secondary cluster provides contextual background on iodine as a dietary mineral (including iodized salt) and its clinical importance to RAI effectiveness.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"RAI therapy is the central intervention tying together cancer subtype, iodine avidity, and imaging workflow\",\n      \"explanation\": \"RAI THERAPY is described as a treatment used for thyroid cancers that are capable of taking up iodine and is particularly recommended for iodine-avid metastatic differentiated thyroid cancers, explicitly including papillary, follicular, and oncocytic subtypes. This makes RAI THERAPY the hub that connects disease biology (iodine avidity) with therapeutic decision-making. [Data: Entities (5072)]\\n\\nIn practice, RAI THERAPY is directly linked to whole-body radioiodine scanning as a tool used before and/or after therapy depending